==== Start a Trip

`POST /api/v1/trips/{tripId}/start`

Called by the driver's app when the passenger has boarded and the trip is physically underway. This is a critical lifecycle gate — the platform validates that the trip is in `DRIVER_ARRIVED` state before allowing the start. Transitioning to `IN_PROGRESS` activates route recording in the Telemetry Service: from this point forward, every GPS coordinate the driver app submits is associated with this trip and used for post-trip fare calculation.

The start timestamp is recorded as the official trip start time and is used in driver behavioral metrics (on-time rate), fare time components, and compliance reporting.

NOTE: If a pre-trip chat session was open between the driver and passenger, it is automatically closed when the trip starts. No further messages can be sent during the trip.

.Business rules
[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| Required prior state | Trip must be in `DRIVER_ARRIVED` state. Attempting to start a trip in any other state (e.g. `DRIVER_ASSIGNED`) returns `400`
| Driver identity | The JWT token must belong to the driver assigned to this trip. A different driver cannot start someone else's trip — this returns `403`
| Idempotency | Calling `/start` on a trip already in `IN_PROGRESS` returns the current trip state without error
|===

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | Trip is not in `DRIVER_ARRIVED` state | Check trip status before calling; the driver must arrive at pickup first
| 403 | Authenticated driver is not the assigned driver for this trip | Confirm the correct driver is logged in
| 404 | Trip ID does not exist | Verify the trip ID
|===

==== Complete a Trip

`POST /api/v1/trips/{tripId}/complete`

Signals that the driver has delivered the passenger to the destination and the journey is over. This endpoint does more than change a status field — it submits the actual GPS path driven to the Pricing Service for final fare computation and triggers the payment collection sequence.

The `actualPath` parameter is an ordered array of `[latitude, longitude]` coordinate pairs representing the exact route driven. The Pricing Service uses this path to: measure the true distance (which may differ from the estimated route due to traffic diversions or driver decisions), detect any toll gates the vehicle passed through, and apply any zone-based rate adjustments for the areas covered.

The trip transitions to `PAYMENT_PENDING` immediately and then to `COMPLETED` once payment clears. If payment fails, the trip moves to `FAILED` and the support team is notified.

.Business rules
[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| Required prior state | Trip must be in `IN_PROGRESS`. Completing a trip that has not started returns `400`
| actualPath requirement | At minimum, the start (pickup) and end (drop-off) coordinates must be included. A path with fewer than 2 coordinate pairs is rejected
| Fare comparison | If the actual fare deviates more than 20% above the original estimate, the platform flags the trip for review before charging. The passenger is notified of the variance
| Driver identity | Only the assigned driver can complete the trip
|===

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | Trip is not in `IN_PROGRESS` state | Verify trip is actively underway before completing
| 400 | `actualPath` contains fewer than 2 coordinate pairs | Submit the full GPS trace from the trip
| 403 | Authenticated user is not the assigned driver | Verify driver identity
| 404 | Trip ID not found | Confirm the trip ID
| 503 | Pricing Service unavailable — fare cannot be calculated | Retry; the trip will remain in `IN_PROGRESS` until pricing resolves
|===

==== Cancel a Trip

`POST /api/v1/trips/{tripId}/cancel`

Cancels an active trip at any point before `COMPLETED`. Can be initiated by the passenger, the driver, or the platform itself (e.g. dispatch timeout, safety intervention). The reason code and optional comments are permanently recorded against the trip for compliance, dispute resolution, and driver behavioral scoring.

Cancellation timing affects driver penalties: cancellations after `DRIVER_ASSIGNED` count against the driver's cancellation rate in behavioral scoring. Cancellations by the platform (e.g. expired, failed) are excluded from driver metrics.

.Business rules
[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| Cancellable states | A trip can be cancelled from any state except `COMPLETED`, `CANCELLED`, or `FAILED`. Attempting to cancel a completed trip returns `400`
| Reason code required | The `reason` query parameter is mandatory. Calls without a reason are rejected with `400`
| Driver cancellation rate | If the cancelling party is the driver and the trip was in `DRIVER_ASSIGNED` or later state, this cancellation is recorded in the driver's behavioral score
| Passenger no-show | When reason is `NO_SHOW` and the trip was in `DRIVER_ARRIVED` state, the driver may be eligible for a waiting time fee depending on operator policy
| Refunds | Cancellations before `DRIVER_ASSIGNED` receive a full refund. Post-assignment cancellations may be subject to a cancellation fee per operator configuration
|===

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | `reason` parameter is missing | Always supply a cancellation reason
| 400 | Trip is already `COMPLETED`, `CANCELLED`, or `FAILED` | Cannot cancel a terminal-state trip
| 403 | Caller is not the assigned driver, the booking passenger, or an admin | Verify identity and permissions
| 404 | Trip ID not found | Confirm the trip ID
|===

==== Get Trip Details

`GET /api/v1/trips/{tripId}`

Returns the full current state of a trip at the moment of the call. This is the canonical source of truth for a trip's status, fare data, assignment details, and scheduling configuration. Both driver and passenger apps poll this endpoint to refresh trip state, though real-time status changes are also pushed via the notification service to reduce polling frequency.

The response includes both `estimatedFare` (computed at booking time) and `actualFare` (computed at completion). Before the trip completes, `actualFare` will be null. After completion, both values are present for fare comparison and dispute resolution.

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 404 | No trip exists with the given ID | Verify the trip ID; trips are not deleted, so a 404 is definitive
| 403 | Caller is not the passenger, assigned driver, or admin | Verify authentication and permissions
|===

==== Get Passenger Trip History

`GET /api/v1/trips/user/{userId}`

Returns all trips ever associated with a passenger account — completed, cancelled, scheduled, and in-progress — ordered newest first. The response is not paginated; for passengers with very large trip histories, clients should consider filtering by date range on the client side.

This endpoint is used to build trip history screens in the passenger app, generate user-level audit trails for support investigations, and produce ridership reports for regulatory submissions.

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 404 | No user account found for the given ID | Verify the user ID against the identity service
| 403 | Caller does not have permission to view this user's trips | Admin or the account owner only
|===

=== Trip Response Fields

The `TripResponseDTO` is returned by all trip lifecycle endpoints. Fields that are not yet populated for the current state will be `null` rather than absent.

[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Field | Type | Description
| `id` | integer | Unique trip identifier assigned at creation. Immutable for the lifetime of the trip
| `userId` | integer | Passenger account ID. Identifies who booked the trip
| `driverId` | integer | The driver assigned to this trip. `null` until a driver accepts the dispatch offer. Set to the accepting driver's ID at `DRIVER_ASSIGNED` state
| `status` | enum | Current lifecycle state. Always reflects the server's authoritative state — do not cache or assume state on the client
| `sourceLatitude` | number | Pickup point latitude as submitted at booking time
| `sourceLongitude` | number | Pickup point longitude as submitted at booking time
| `sourceAddress` | string | Human-readable pickup address if provided at booking. May be `null` if only coordinates were supplied
| `destinationLatitude` | number | Drop-off point latitude
| `destinationLongitude` | number | Drop-off point longitude
| `destinationAddress` | string | Human-readable destination address if provided
| `vehicleType` | enum | Vehicle category requested at booking time
| `paymentMethod` | enum | How this trip's fare will be collected
| `paymentStatus` | enum | Current payment state. `PENDING` during the trip, `PROCESSING` after completion, `COMPLETED` once charged, `FAILED` if payment could not be collected, `REFUNDED` if a cancellation refund was issued
| `estimatedFare` | number | The fare estimate generated at booking time. Reflects zone rates, time window, and surge at the moment the trip was booked. This value does not change after booking
| `actualFare` | number | The fare calculated after trip completion using the actual route driven. `null` until the trip reaches `PAYMENT_PENDING`. May differ from `estimatedFare` due to route deviations, actual travel time, or toll gates encountered
| `isScheduled` | boolean | `true` for trips that were pre-booked with a `scheduledTime`. `false` for immediate trips
| `scheduledTime` | datetime | The requested departure time for scheduled trips. `null` for immediate bookings
| `isRecurring` | boolean | `true` if this trip is one instance in a recurring series
| `requestedAt` | datetime | ISO-8601 timestamp of when the booking request was received by the platform
| `completedAt` | datetime | ISO-8601 timestamp of when the trip reached `COMPLETED` state. `null` for in-progress or cancelled trips
|===

