== Telemetry API

*Base URL:* `http://localhost:10002`

The Telemetry Service is the real-time geospatial backbone of the Tanzim dispatch platform. Its primary responsibilities are: ingesting a continuous stream of GPS position updates from thousands of simultaneously active driver devices; maintaining a low-latency geospatial index that the dispatch engine queries to find the nearest eligible driver when a passenger books a trip; and serving live driver positions to passenger apps so riders can watch their driver's icon move toward the pickup point in real time.

Unlike the other services — which are backed by relational databases and optimised for durable, query-rich data — the Telemetry Service is deliberately designed around an in-memory Redis geospatial store. Driver positions are treated as ephemeral streaming data, not records. Every update overwrites the previous position for that driver. The geospatial index is rebuilt from live pings, not from persisted history. This architecture allows the service to sustain write throughput far beyond what a relational database could handle at GPS ping frequency across a large active fleet.

The service exposes two categories of endpoints: **ingestion endpoints** (high-frequency writes from the driver app) and **query endpoints** (reads by the dispatch engine, passenger app, and operations dashboard). These two categories have very different traffic patterns and the API is designed accordingly — the simple coordinate ping endpoint (`/update/simple`) is as lightweight as possible to minimise the cost of thousands of concurrent writes per second.

=== Location Data Architecture

[source]
----
Driver App (every 4-10s)
    │
    ▼ POST /locations/update/simple  (coordinates only — high frequency)
    │ POST /locations/update         (full state — on state changes)
    │
    ▼
Redis Geospatial Index
    │
    ├──▶ Dispatch Engine  (GET /locations/nearest)       → trip assignment
    ├──▶ Passenger App    (GET /locations/nearby/available) → live map
    └──▶ Ops Dashboard    (GET /locations/available/count)  → fleet monitoring
----

=== Location Update Strategy

The service provides two update paths because driver apps need to send GPS pings very frequently but do not change their full state on every ping. Sending a full state payload at GPS frequency would waste bandwidth and increase processing load unnecessarily.

[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Endpoint | When to call | What it updates
| `POST /locations/update/simple` | Every 4–10 seconds while active | Coordinates only — the geospatial index position
| `POST /locations/update` | When state changes (availability, trip start/end, going online/offline) | Full location record including availability, trip ID, vehicle ID, bearing, speed, battery level
|===

NOTE: Calling `/update` at GPS-ping frequency is not recommended. The simple endpoint exists precisely to avoid this. A full update on every ping would saturate the service and degrade performance for all consumers of the geospatial index.

=== Business Rules

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| Coordinate bounds | Latitude must be between -90.0 and 90.0. Longitude must be between -180.0 and 180.0. Requests outside these bounds are rejected with `400`
| Driver identity enforcement | Location update endpoints validate that the `driverId` in the request matches the authenticated driver's JWT. A driver cannot submit location data for a different driver. Admin tokens bypass this check
| Bearing range | Bearing values must be between 0.0 and 360.0 degrees. Values outside this range are rejected
| Battery level range | Battery level must be an integer between 0 and 100. Values outside this range are rejected
| Geospatial search radius cap | The nearby search endpoints enforce a maximum search radius of 50 km. Requests with `radiusKm` above 50 are clamped to 50 km rather than rejected, to prevent overly broad searches from degrading index performance
| Stale data handling | There is no automatic TTL on driver locations. A driver who goes offline without calling the remove endpoint will remain in the index with a stale `lastUpdated` timestamp. The dispatch engine uses the `lastUpdated` field to filter out drivers whose last ping was more than a configurable threshold ago (default: 5 minutes)
|===

=== Location Management Endpoints

==== Update Driver Location (Full)

`POST /api/v1/locations/update`

Full location update including speed, bearing, accuracy, availability, and current trip context. Call this when the driver's state changes meaningfully — going online, accepting a trip, becoming unavailable, changing vehicle. Also use this as a periodic heartbeat every 20–60 seconds to ensure the dispatch engine's view of the driver's state stays fresh even if no state changes have occurred.

.Business rules
[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| Coordinate validation | Latitude and longitude are mandatory and must be within valid geographic bounds. Bearing must be 0–360 if supplied. Speed must be non-negative
| Driver identity | `driverId` in the request body must match the driver in the JWT. Mismatches return `403`
| Availability flag | If `isAvailable` is `false`, the driver is removed from the available pool in the geospatial index and will not appear in dispatch searches until availability is set back to `true`
| Trip linkage | When `currentTripId` is supplied, the update is associated with that trip's route recording. Route coordinates submitted while `currentTripId` is set are used for post-trip fare calculation
|===

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | Invalid or out-of-bounds coordinates | Check latitude (-90–90) and longitude (-180–180) values
| 400 | Bearing outside 0–360 range | Supply a valid compass bearing
| 403 | `driverId` does not match authenticated driver | Use the driver's own token; do not share tokens between devices
| 404 | Driver not found in the system | Verify the driver ID exists in the Driver Service
| 503 | Redis geospatial store unavailable | Retry with exponential backoff; the driver app should queue updates locally
|===

==== Simple Location Update (High Frequency)

`POST /api/v1/locations/update/simple?driverId={id}&latitude={lat}&longitude={lon}`

The most frequently called endpoint in the entire platform. No request body — all data is in query parameters. This intentional design minimises HTTP overhead for a call that may be made every 4–10 seconds per active driver.

This endpoint *only* updates the driver's position in the geospatial index. It does not update availability, speed, bearing, battery level, or trip assignment. Those fields retain whatever values were last set by a full `/update` call.

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | Invalid coordinates | Check latitude and longitude bounds
| 204 | Success — no body returned | This is the expected success response; do not treat the absence of a body as an error
| 503 | Redis unavailable | Queue updates locally and replay when service recovers
|===

==== Update Driver Availability

`PUT /api/v1/locations/driver/{driverId}/availability?isAvailable={bool}`

Sets the driver's availability flag in the telemetry index without requiring a full location payload. The availability flag controls whether the driver appears in dispatch search results. Setting `isAvailable=false` immediately removes the driver from all nearest-driver and nearby-available queries.

Use this for instantaneous state toggles: when a driver accepts or completes a trip, the Trip Service calls this endpoint to flip availability before the next dispatch cycle runs.

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | `isAvailable` parameter missing or not a boolean | Supply `true` or `false`
| 404 | Driver not currently tracked (no location record exists) | Driver must submit at least one location update before availability can be set
| 403 | Caller does not have permission to update this driver's availability | Use the driver's own token or an admin token
|===

==== Get Driver Location

`GET /api/v1/locations/driver/{driverId}`

Returns the last known full location record for a driver — their coordinates, availability, current trip, speed, bearing, battery level, and the timestamp of the last update. This is the definitive source for any consumer that needs to know where a specific driver is right now.

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 404 | Driver has no location record in the index | The driver has not submitted any location updates — they may be offline or their device has not yet pinged
|===

==== Remove Driver from Tracking

`DELETE /api/v1/locations/driver/{driverId}`

Removes the driver's entry from the geospatial index entirely. After this call the driver will return 404 from the Get Location endpoint and will not appear in any nearby or nearest searches. This is the correct endpoint to call when a driver ends their shift and goes offline — it keeps the index clean and prevents stale entries from being returned in dispatch queries.

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 204 | Successfully removed — no body | This is the expected success response
| 404 | Driver was not in the tracking index | No action needed — idempotent removal
|===

==== Batch Update Driver Locations

`POST /api/v1/locations/batch-update`

Updates multiple driver locations in a single API call. Used by fleet management systems and operator backend integrations that aggregate GPS data from their own tracking devices before forwarding to Tanzim.

==== Get Multiple Driver Locations

`POST /api/v1/locations/drivers/batch`

Retrieves current location data for a list of driver IDs in one request. More efficient than individual calls when the passenger app or dispatch engine needs to refresh several drivers' positions simultaneously.

==== Batch Remove Driver Locations

