== Pricing API

*Base URL:* `http://localhost:10003`

The Pricing Service is the financial calculation engine of the Tanzim platform. It is responsible for two distinct but related tasks: generating the **fare estimate** shown to a passenger before they book, and computing the **actual fare** charged at trip completion. These are not the same calculation — the estimate is a projection based on the planned route, while the actual fare is computed from the GPS coordinates the driver submitted during the trip.

The pricing model is deliberately layered. A base rate applies to every trip, but multiple overlapping factors can modify it. A trip that departs from an airport special zone, travels through a toll gate, and is booked during the morning peak hour will have contributions from zone pricing, toll charges, and time-window pricing all applied simultaneously. The `pricingFactors` array in every estimate response makes this composition fully transparent — every factor that affected the fare is listed with its value and impact.

This transparency is important for passenger trust and for dispute resolution. When a passenger questions their fare, the support team can retrieve the estimate response and point to exactly which factors were applied and why.

The service also manages the configuration objects that define those factors: pricing zones (geofenced areas with rate overrides), toll gates (physical infrastructure with time-based charges), and time windows (scheduling rules that define peak, off-peak, and late-night rate periods). Each of these configuration types has its own CRUD API and can be toggled on/off independently without disrupting other pricing rules.

=== Fare Calculation Formula

[source]
----
Step 1 — Base components:
    subtotal = onboardingFare
             + (distanceKm × perKmRate)
             + (durationMinutes × perMinuteRate)

Step 2 — Add fixed charges:
    subtotal = subtotal + tollCharges + bookingFee + serviceTax

Step 3 — Apply minimum fare floor:
    subtotal = max(subtotal, minimumFare)

Step 4 — Apply surge multiplier:
    totalFare = subtotal × surgeMultiplier

----

The `perKmRate`, `onboardingFare`, and `perMinuteRate` used in Step 1 are sourced from whichever pricing configuration takes priority: zone pricing overrides time-window pricing, which overrides the base rate. The highest-priority matching configuration wins.

=== Pricing Priority Order

[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Priority | Source | Description
| 1 (Highest) | Zone pricing rule | Rate defined for a specific zone and vehicle type combination. Applied when the pickup or destination falls within an active special pricing zone
| 2 | Time window pricing | Rate defined for a time window (e.g. PEAK) and vehicle type. Applied when the booking time falls within an active window
| 3 (Lowest) | Base rate | The fallback rate if no zone or time window applies
|===

=== Pricing Zones

Zones are polygon-defined geographic areas that override the base per-km and onboarding fare rates within their boundaries. When the pickup point or destination of a trip falls within a zone, that zone's rates are applied instead of the default time-window or base rates. If both pickup and destination are in different zones, the zone with the higher priority value wins.

Zones are stored as closed polygons — the coordinate array must define at least 3 vertices. The platform automatically closes the polygon (connecting the last vertex back to the first) if the final coordinate is not identical to the first.

==== Zone Types

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Type | Description
| `SPECIAL_PRICING` | Areas with permanently adjusted fares regardless of time of day. Used for tourist districts, corporate parks, gated communities, and other locations where service demand or operating costs justify a different rate structure
| `TOLL_GATE` | Zones that are managed alongside toll gate infrastructure. Setting a zone as `TOLL_GATE` type links it to toll gate records for coordinated activation and deactivation
| `SURGE_ZONE` | Geographic areas where demand-supply imbalance triggers automatic fare multipliers. Surge zones are evaluated in real time — the system monitors driver availability relative to request volume within the zone boundary
| `RESTRICTED` | Areas where Tanzim service is prohibited or subject to special operator agreements. Trips with origins or destinations inside a `RESTRICTED` zone are blocked at the booking stage
|===

==== Zone Pricing Rule Fields

When adding a pricing rule to a zone, the following fields apply:

[cols="1,1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Field | Type | Required | Description
| `zoneId` | integer | Yes | The zone this rule applies to
| `vehicleType` | enum | Yes | Which vehicle category this rate applies to. Each vehicle type requires its own rule within the same zone — there is no wildcard
| `onboardingFare` | number | Yes | Fixed boarding charge within this zone, replacing the default onboarding fare. Must be ≥ 0
| `perKmRate` | number | Yes | Per-kilometre rate within this zone. Must be ≥ 0. Applied to the full trip distance if the pickup is in the zone
| `active` | boolean | No | Whether this rule is currently enforced. Defaults to `true`
| `priority` | integer | No | Resolution order when multiple zone rules could apply. Higher number = higher priority. Defaults to 0
|===

==== Zone Business Rules

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| Polygon minimum | A zone polygon must have at least 3 coordinate pairs. Fewer than 3 points cannot form a closed area and are rejected with `400`
| Vehicle-type specificity | Each pricing rule within a zone is bound to a single vehicle type. To cover all vehicle types, a separate rule must be created for each. There is no "apply to all types" shorthand
| Zone overlap | Overlapping zones are permitted. When a trip falls in multiple zones simultaneously, the zone rule with the highest `priority` value is applied. Ties are broken by zone ID (lower ID wins)
| Restricted zone enforcement | If a trip's source or destination falls inside a `RESTRICTED` zone, the booking request is rejected by the Trip Service with a `400` error. The client should surface a message to the passenger explaining that service is unavailable at that location
|===

.Error scenarios for zone operations
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | Fewer than 3 coordinate pairs in the polygon | Add more coordinate pairs to form a valid polygon
| 400 | Coordinate out of bounds (lat > 90 or lon > 180, etc.) | Correct the coordinate values
| 404 | Zone ID not found | Verify the zone ID exists
| 404 | Vehicle type rule not found for zone | Create a zone pricing rule for that vehicle type first
|===

==== Create Pricing Zone

`POST /api/v1/pricing/zones`

Defines a new geofenced pricing zone using a polygon coordinate array (minimum 3 points to close the polygon).

[source,json]
----
{
  "name": "Dubai International Airport Zone",
  "zoneType": "SPECIAL_PRICING",
  "description": "Premium pickup and drop-off rates apply within DXB airport boundaries",
  "coordinates": [
    { "latitude": 25.2532, "longitude": 55.3657 },
    { "latitude": 25.2532, "longitude": 55.4100 },
    { "latitude": 25.2100, "longitude": 55.4100 },
