=== Toll Gates

Toll gates represent physical road toll collection points on routes within the Tanzim operating area. When the platform calculates a fare estimate, it traces the planned route and checks each toll gate's polygon to determine whether the route passes through any managed toll infrastructure. Every toll gate detected on the route contributes its charge — based on vehicle type and time of day — to the `tollCharges` component of the fare.

Toll gate polygons are typically narrow rectangles positioned perpendicular to the road at the toll point, sized to capture the road footprint while excluding adjacent areas. Each toll gate has a unique `code` (e.g. `SZR-TG-03`) used for integration with external toll management systems.

==== Toll Gate Pricing Rule Fields

[cols="1,1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Field | Type | Required | Description
| `tollGateId` | integer | Yes | The toll gate this rule applies to
| `vehicleType` | enum | Yes | Which vehicle category this charge applies to
| `tollCharge` | number | Yes | The charge amount in the operating currency. Must be ≥ 0
| `startTime` | LocalTime | No | Start of the time window during which this charge applies. If not supplied, the charge applies at all times
| `endTime` | LocalTime | No | End of the time window. Must be after `startTime` if both are supplied
| `daysOfWeek` | array of integers | No | Days this rule is active (1=Monday through 7=Sunday). If not supplied, the rule applies every day
| `active` | boolean | No | Whether this pricing rule is currently enforced. Defaults to `true`
| `description` | string | No | Human-readable notes about this pricing rule (e.g. "Peak weekday rate", "Weekend flat rate")
|===

==== Toll Gate Business Rules

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| Multiple rules per gate | A single toll gate can have multiple pricing rules for different vehicle types and/or time periods. The platform selects the first active matching rule at the time of booking. If no rule matches the vehicle type and time, no toll charge is applied for that gate
| Route detection | Toll gate detection is performed by checking whether any segment of the estimated route intersects the toll gate's polygon. A route that bypasses the toll gate's physical location will not incur its charge
| Pre-trip vs actual | Toll charges in the fare estimate are based on the planned route. The actual fare (computed at trip completion from the GPS path) may include different toll gates if the driver took a different route
| Zero-charge gates | A toll gate with `tollCharge: 0` is valid and can be used to document infrastructure that does not charge (e.g. a monitored checkpoint)
|===

.Error scenarios for toll gate operations
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | `tollCharge` is negative | Toll charges must be zero or positive
| 400 | `endTime` is before `startTime` | Time window end must follow start
| 400 | Polygon has fewer than 3 coordinate pairs | Add more vertices to form a valid polygon
| 404 | Toll gate ID not found | Verify the toll gate ID
| 409 | A toll gate with the given `code` already exists | Codes must be unique; use a distinct code for each physical gate
|===

==== Create Toll Gate

`POST /api/v1/pricing/toll-gates`

Registers a new toll gate with a polygon boundary and unique code.

[source,json]
----
{
  "name": "Sheikh Zayed Road Toll Gate 3",
  "code": "SZR-TG-03",
  "description": "Inbound toll at Interchange 3, SZR",
  "coordinates": [
    { "latitude": 25.1812, "longitude": 55.2605 },
    { "latitude": 25.1812, "longitude": 55.2650 },
    { "latitude": 25.1780, "longitude": 55.2650 },
    { "latitude": 25.1780, "longitude": 55.2605 }
  ],
  "active": true
}
----

==== Add Toll Pricing Rule

`POST /api/v1/pricing/toll-gates/{tollGateId}/pricing`

Attaches a vehicle-type and time-based pricing rule to a toll gate. Toll charges can differ by vehicle type and time of day.

[source,json]
----
{
  "tollGateId": 12,
  "vehicleType": "SEDAN",
  "tollCharge": 4.00,
  "startTime": { "hour": 6, "minute": 0, "second": 0, "nano": 0 },
  "endTime": { "hour": 22, "minute": 0, "second": 0, "nano": 0 },
  "daysOfWeek": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],
  "active": true,
  "description": "Weekday daytime rate"
}
----

==== Calculate Toll Charge

`GET /api/v1/pricing/toll-gates/{tollGateId}/calculate?vehicleType={type}&time={datetime}`

Calculates the applicable toll charge for a given vehicle type at a given time (defaults to now). Used during pre-trip estimation when the planned route passes through a known toll gate.

==== Get Toll Gate by Code

`GET /api/v1/pricing/toll-gates/by-code/{code}`

Looks up a toll gate by its unique code. Used by the route-analysis engine to resolve toll gate references from mapping data.

=== Time Windows

Time windows define the pricing periods that apply across the operating day. They are the mechanism through which Tanzim implements peak-hour pricing, late-night incentive rates, and weekend flat rates. At the moment a fare estimate is generated, the platform looks up which time window (if any) is active for the given vehicle type at the requested departure time, and applies that window's rates in place of the default base rate.

Each time window is specific to a single vehicle type — there is no cross-type shorthand. To configure peak pricing for both `SEDAN` and `SUV`, two separate time window records must be created. Windows can overlap on the clock (e.g. a `REGULAR` window from 09:00–18:00 and a `PEAK` window from 07:00–09:00) — the `priority` field determines which window wins when overlap occurs.

==== Time Window Field Descriptions

[cols="1,1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Field | Type | Required | Description
| `name` | string | Yes | Human-readable label (e.g. "Morning Peak - Sedan", "Late Night SUV"). Used in fare breakdowns shown to passengers
| `vehicleType` | enum | Yes | The vehicle category this window applies to
| `windowType` | enum | Yes | Classification: `PEAK`, `OFF_PEAK`, `REGULAR`, `LATE_NIGHT`, or `WEEKEND`. Used for reporting and display only — does not affect pricing logic
| `startTime` | LocalTime | Yes | When this pricing window begins (e.g. `{ "hour": 7, "minute": 0 }`)
| `endTime` | LocalTime | Yes | When this pricing window ends. If `endTime` is before `startTime` (e.g. 23:00 to 02:00), the window is understood to span midnight
| `daysOfWeek` | array of integers | No | Days this window is active. 1 = Monday, 7 = Sunday. If empty or not supplied, the window is active every day
| `onboardingFare` | number | Yes | Fixed boarding charge applied during this window. Must be ≥ 0
| `perKmRate` | number | Yes | Per-kilometre rate during this window. Must be ≥ 0
| `perMinuteRate` | number | Yes | Per-minute rate during this window. Must be ≥ 0. Applied to estimated travel time in the pre-trip estimate and to actual trip duration in the post-trip fare
| `minimumFare` | number | Yes | Floor fare — no trip booked in this window will be charged less than this amount, even if the calculated fare is lower. Must be ≥ 0
| `active` | boolean | No | Whether this window is currently enforced. Defaults to `true`
| `priority` | integer | No | Determines which window applies when multiple windows could match the same vehicle type and time. Higher number = higher priority. Defaults to 0
|===

==== Time Window Business Rules

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| One active window per query | The `GET /time-windows/active` endpoint returns at most one window per vehicle type at a given time. If multiple active windows match, the one with the highest `priority` is returned
| Midnight-spanning windows | Windows where `endTime` is earlier on the clock than `startTime` are treated as spanning midnight (e.g. 22:00–02:00 covers the late-night period into the next calendar day)
| Gap between windows | If no active window matches the booking time and vehicle type, the base rate is used. It is valid to leave time gaps — not every minute of the day needs a window configured
| Priority ties | If two windows have the same priority and both match the same vehicle type and time, the window with the lower database ID is selected. Configure distinct priority values to avoid ambiguity
|===

.Error scenarios for time window operations
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | `onboardingFare`, `perKmRate`, `perMinuteRate`, or `minimumFare` is negative | All fare amounts must be zero or positive
| 400 | `startTime` and `endTime` are identical | A window must span at least one minute
| 400 | Missing required time or rate fields | Ensure all required fields are present
| 404 | Time window ID not found | Verify the window ID
|===

==== Create Time Window

`POST /api/v1/pricing/time-windows`

Defines a new pricing window with the applicable rates.

[source,json]
----
{
  "name": "Morning Peak - Sedan",
  "vehicleType": "SEDAN",
  "windowType": "PEAK",
  "startTime": { "hour": 7, "minute": 0, "second": 0, "nano": 0 },
  "endTime":   { "hour": 9, "minute": 30, "second": 0, "nano": 0 },
  "daysOfWeek": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],
  "onboardingFare": 5.00,
  "perKmRate": 2.80,
  "perMinuteRate": 0.35,
  "minimumFare": 12.00,
  "active": true,
  "priority": 20
}
----

==== Get Active Time Window

`GET /api/v1/pricing/time-windows/active?vehicleType={type}&time={datetime}`

Returns whichever time window is currently active for the given vehicle type at the given moment (defaults to now). The pricing engine calls this at every trip estimate to select the correct rate set.

=== Fare Estimation Endpoints

==== Calculate Price Estimate

`POST /api/v1/pricing/estimate`

The primary pre-trip fare estimation endpoint. Given a pickup point, destination, and vehicle type, the engine executes the full pricing pipeline:

1. Queries the mapping integration to calculate route distance (km) and estimated travel time (minutes) for the given coordinates
2. Checks the pickup and destination points against all active pricing zones to identify any zone-based rate overrides
3. Determines which time window (if any) is active for this vehicle type at the current time
4. Traces the planned route against all active toll gate polygons to identify any gates on the path
5. Applies zone or time-window rate overrides — zone pricing takes precedence over time-window pricing
6. Computes the fare components: onboarding fare, distance fare, time fare, toll charges, booking fee, and service tax
7. Applies the minimum fare floor
8. Applies any active surge multiplier for the pickup zone
9. Returns the total fare, a min/max fare range, and a full breakdown of every applied factor

The returned `estimateId` should be stored by the client and passed when creating a trip booking. The Trip Service uses this ID to record which estimate the passenger was shown at booking time. Estimates expire — typically after 10 minutes — and a new estimate must be generated if booking is delayed.

.Business rules
[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Detail
| Estimate expiry | Estimates are valid for a configurable window (default 10 minutes). After expiry the estimate is retained in the database but `isPriceEstimateValid` returns `false`. Expired estimates should not be used for trip booking — generate a fresh one
| Fare range | The response includes `estimatedFareMin` and `estimatedFareMax` representing a confidence interval around `totalFare`. The range widens in high-surge or ambiguous-route conditions
| Surge transparency | If `surgeMultiplier > 1.0`, the `surgeReason` field always contains a human-readable explanation suitable for display to the passenger (e.g. "High demand near Dubai Mall")
| Restricted zone blocking | If the pickup or destination falls inside a `RESTRICTED` pricing zone, the estimate request is rejected with `400`. The client should surface the restriction message to the passenger
| Minimum fare | The `minimumFare` from the active time window or zone pricing rule acts as a floor. No estimate will return a `totalFare` below the applicable minimum
|===

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 400 | Missing required coordinates or `vehicleType` | Supply all required fields
| 400 | Pickup or destination is in a `RESTRICTED` zone | Inform the passenger that service is unavailable at this location
| 400 | Coordinates out of geographic bounds | Verify latitude and longitude values
| 503 | Mapping service unavailable — route distance cannot be calculated | Retry; the estimate cannot be generated without routing data
| 503 | Pricing configuration not loaded — no base rates found | Contact platform support; rate configuration may be missing
|===

==== Quick Price Estimate

`GET /api/v1/pricing/estimate/quick?sourceLat={}&sourceLon={}&destLat={}&destLon={}&vehicleType={}`

Lightweight GET-based estimate for simple integrations and price-check widgets. Returns the same full estimate response without requiring a request body. Identical calculation pipeline to `POST /estimate` — use this when constructing a request body is impractical (e.g. a link-based price widget, a browser bookmarklet, or server-side rendering without a POST client).

NOTE: Because all parameters are in the URL, this endpoint's requests appear in server logs. Do not use it for requests where the coordinates should remain private.

==== Calculate Prices for All Vehicle Types

`POST /api/v1/pricing/estimate/all`

Executes the full pricing pipeline once per vehicle type and returns estimates for all supported types in a single response. Used by the booking screen to display the vehicle selection carousel with prices side-by-side. The response includes a `calculatedAt` timestamp so clients can display fare freshness to the passenger.

NOTE: This endpoint invokes the full pricing engine for every vehicle type. It is more expensive than a single-type estimate — avoid calling it for non-UI purposes such as backend batch processing.

==== Get Fare Breakdown (Pre-Trip)

`POST /api/v1/pricing/estimate/fare-breakdown`

Returns a detailed line-item breakdown for a route where the distance and travel time are already known. Use this when the client has already fetched the route from a mapping API and does not want the Pricing Service to recalculate routing. Supplying the route as a `lineString` (ordered coordinate array) allows the service to perform toll gate detection without re-routing.

==== Get Actual Fare Breakdown (Post-Trip)

`POST /api/v1/pricing/estimate/fare-breakdown-actual`

Computes the definitive post-trip fare from the GPS coordinates the driver submitted during the trip. This is the amount that will be charged to the passenger. The `coordinates` parameter should be the same coordinate array passed to `POST /trips/{tripId}/complete`.

The actual fare may differ from the pre-trip estimate for three reasons: the driver may have taken a longer or shorter route than the planned path; the actual trip duration may differ from the estimated duration; or the driver may have passed through different toll gates than were predicted on the planned route.

==== Retrieve Saved Estimate

`GET /api/v1/pricing/estimate/{estimateId}`

Retrieves a previously generated estimate by its ID. The estimate is retained even after expiry — it will be returned with its original values but `isPriceEstimateValid` will return `false` for expired estimates. This allows support teams to reconstruct exactly what fare was shown to a passenger at booking time when investigating a dispute.

.Error scenarios
[cols="1,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| HTTP Status | Condition | Resolution
| 404 | Estimate ID not found | Verify the estimate ID; IDs are generated at estimation time and cannot be constructed manually
|===

==== Check Estimate Validity

`GET /api/v1/pricing/estimate/{estimateId}/valid`

Returns a boolean. `true` means the estimate was generated within the validity window and its rates are still current. `false` means the estimate has expired and a new one should be generated before the passenger completes their booking.

==== Calculate Distance and Time Only

`POST /api/v1/pricing/distance-time`

Queries the mapping integration for route distance and estimated travel time without applying any pricing logic. Returns distance in both kilometres and miles, and travel time as both a raw integer (minutes) and a human-readable display string (e.g. "23 mins"). Use this when the app needs routing data for display purposes without the overhead of a full fare calculation.

=== Surge Pricing Endpoints

==== Get Current Surge Multiplier

`GET /api/v1/pricing/surge?vehicleType={type}`

Returns the active surge multiplier for a vehicle type at the current moment. A multiplier of `1.0` means no surge — fares are at standard rates. Values above `1.0` indicate that demand in the area is outpacing driver supply and fares have been elevated to attract more drivers online and moderate demand.

Surge conditions are evaluated in real time by comparing the count of available drivers in each pricing zone against the rate of incoming trip requests. The multiplier, the zone(s) driving the surge, and the reason string are all returned so the passenger-facing UI can display a meaningful explanation.

.Response fields (`SurgePricingResponse`)
[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Field | Description
| `vehicleType` | The vehicle type the surge applies to
| `surgeMultiplier` | Current multiplier (e.g. `1.5` = fares are 50% above standard)
| `message` | Human-readable reason for display to the passenger (e.g. "High demand near your location. Prices are temporarily higher.")
| `effectiveAt` | Timestamp when this surge level became active
|===

